## `buildBodyInserter`

```java
public BodyInserter<?, ?> buildBodyInserter(Object body, String contentType, Boolean encodeParamsToggle) {
    if (body == null) {
        return BodyInserters.fromValue(new byte[0]);
    }
    switch (contentType) {
        case MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE:
            final Object bodyObject = parseJsonBody(body);
            return BodyInserters.fromValue(bodyObject);
        case MediaType.APPLICATION_FORM_URLENCODED_VALUE:
            final String formData = parseFormData((List<Property>) body, encodeParamsToggle);
            if ("".equals(formData)) {
                return BodyInserters.fromValue(new byte[0]);
            }
            return BodyInserters.fromValue(formData);
        case MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA_VALUE:
            return parseMultipartFileData((List<Property>) body);
        case MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN_VALUE:
            return BodyInserters.fromValue((String) body);
        case MediaType.APPLICATION_OCTET_STREAM_VALUE:
            return parseMultimediaData((String) body);
        default:
            return BodyInserters.fromValue(((String) body).getBytes(StandardCharsets.ISO_8859_1));
    }
}
```

## `parseJsonBody`

```java
public Object parseJsonBody(Object body) {
    try {
        if (body instanceof String) {
            if ("" == body) {
                return new byte[0];
            }
            Object objectFromJson = objectFromJson((String) body);
            if (objectFromJson != null) {
                body = objectFromJson;
            }
        }
    } catch (JsonSyntaxException | ParseException e) {
        throw new AppsmithPluginException(
                AppsmithPluginError.PLUGIN_JSON_PARSE_ERROR, body, "Malformed JSON: " + e.getMessage());
    }
    return body;
}
```

## How Validity Is Determined & How the Body Is Transmitted

For `APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE`, the body passes through `parseJsonBody`, which:

- Returns an empty byte array (`new byte[0]`) for an empty string body.
- Calls `objectFromJson` to attempt parsing the string into a `Map` or `List`.
- Throws a `PLUGIN_JSON_PARSE_ERROR` on `JsonSyntaxException` or `ParseException`, with the message prefixed `"Malformed JSON: "`.
- If parsing succeeds, the resulting parsed object replaces the raw string; if it returns `null`, the original body is retained.

The result is passed to `BodyInserters.fromValue(bodyObject)`, meaning the JSON is transmitted as a **parsed object** (not raw bytes) when valid — allowing re-serialization by the WebClient. Raw byte transmission (via `getBytes(...)`) only occurs in the `default` fallback branch, not the JSON branch.

### `objectFromJson` — top-level type detection

```java
private static Object objectFromJson(String jsonString) throws ParseException {
    Class<?> type;
    String trimmed = jsonString.trim();

    if (trimmed.startsWith("{")) {
        type = Map.class;
    } else if (trimmed.startsWith("[")) {
        type = List.class;
    } else {
        return null;
    }

    if (type.equals(List.class)) {
        return parseJsonIntoListWithOrderedObjects(jsonString, gson, jsonParser);
    } else {
        return parseJsonIntoOrderedObject(jsonString, gson, jsonParser);
    }
}
```

Only `Map`/`List` top-levels are supported — a string that starts with neither `{` nor `[` returns `null`, causing the raw body to pass through unchanged.

### Fallback parsing strategies

```java
private static Object parseJsonIntoListWithOrderedObjects(String jsonString, Gson gson, JSONParser jsonParser)
        throws ParseException {
    TypeToken<List<Object>> listTypeToken = new TypeToken<>() {};
    try {
        return gson.fromJson(jsonString, listTypeToken.getType());
    } catch (JsonSyntaxException jsonSyntaxException) {
        return jsonParser.parse(jsonString);
    }
}
```

```java
private static Object parseJsonIntoOrderedObject(String jsonString, Gson gson, JSONParser jsonParser)
        throws ParseException {
    TypeToken<LinkedHashMap<String, Object>> linkedHashMapTypeToken = new TypeToken<>() {};
    try {
        return gson.fromJson(jsonString, linkedHashMapTypeToken.getType());
    } catch (JsonSyntaxException jsonSyntaxException) {
        JsonReader jsonReader = new JsonReader();
        CollectionMapper.MapClass<LinkedHashMap<String, Object>> collectionMapper =
                new CollectionMapper.MapClass<>(jsonReader, linkedHashMapTypeToken.getRawType());
        return jsonParser.parse(jsonString, collectionMapper);
    }
}
```

### The Gson instance and its parsing configuration

```java
private static final Gson gson = new GsonBuilder()
        .setLenient()
        .setObjectToNumberStrategy(ToNumberPolicy.LAZILY_PARSED_NUMBER)
        .setNumberToNumberStrategy(ToNumberPolicy.LAZILY_PARSED_NUMBER)
        .create();

private static final JSONParser jsonParser = new JSONParser(JSONParser.MODE_PERMISSIVE);
```

## Summary of the Strict vs. Re-serialization Logic

The code uses a **two-tier parsing approach**. Gson attempts parsing first because, per the comments, it preserves attribute order via LinkedHashMap-based objects. If Gson fails on a `JsonSyntaxException` (e.g., trailing commas that violate strict RFC standards), the `net.minidev` `JSONParser` in permissive mode (`MODE_PERMISSIVE`) is used as a fallback. The Gson builder is configured to be lenient and to use `LAZILY_PARSED_NUMBER` policies so numeric values are preserved rather than being coerced (e.g., avoiding `4` becoming `4.0`). Ordered maps are specifically preserved for use cases like AWS authorization signature headers referenced in the comments.

